    "j_cases_status": "DECLARED ENGINEERING CONSTRAINT CASES. J_1e6 is where 10.3390/ma14216394 observes Cu-Cu bump electromigration under ACCELERATED stress; an accelerated test condition is evidence about failure physics, not a safe operating limit, so it is used here to mark a regime and never as a rating. CONTEXT_ONLY.",
    "selection_status": "ADMISSIBLE_REGION_ONLY",
    "why_no_single_count": "loss is monotonically decreasing in N and voltage drop is satisfied from N = 4 upward, so every criterion available here is one-sided: nothing bounds N from above except geometry. A unique optimum would require a lifetime or current-density allowable for this pad metallurgy, geometry, temperature and waveform, which no accessible source provides. Reporting a single adopted count would be reinstating the withdrawn tautology under a new name.",
    "drop_floor_current_density_A_cm2": 5143257.651240717,
    "drop_floor_verdict": "NON-PHYSICAL. At the voltage-drop floor N = 4 each pad carries 5.143 A through 100 um^2, i.e. 5.14e+06 A/cm^2 -- about 5x the ~1e6 A/cm^2 at which Cu-Cu bump electromigration is studied under ACCELERATED stress. The drop budget is therefore not the binding constraint anywhere in this design space: current density is. The old 'voltage-drop floor of 4 pads/rail/cell' was never a candidate design point, only an artefact of applying one criterion in isolation.",
    "crowding": "OPEN -- j_per_pad is the AVERAGE over the pad area. Local crowding at the pad/UBM transition is not modelled and no multiplier is invented"
